## Reflection on Human-AI Collaboration (400-500 words)

My experience using AI in this course was a process of learning to delegate routine and exploratory tasks while keeping critical judgment central. AI accelerated several routine tasks: it proposed succinct explanations of methodological concepts, suggested debugging steps, and produced alternative phrasing for our presentation. For the literature review, Gemini offered a clear scaffolding that saved hours during the initial drafting phase; however, it did not replace my need to read primary source materials. The AI could suggest theories and frameworks, but it could not determine which theoretical lens best fit the historical and institutional context of the Hong Kong Observatory. That required domain knowledge and a nuanced reading of past reports and public submissions on disaster response.

When using GitHub Copilot to debug Python code, I found it extremely valuable for catching common syntax and logic issues. Its automatic suggestions reduced the time spent on trivial debugging and let me concentrate on modeling decisions—such as which distributional assumptions to adopt for simulation or what predictors to include in a regression. Important to note: I always verified Copilot's suggested changes through test cases and peer-reviewed logic. I added boundary checks and unit tests where Copilot's suggestion was a quick fix but had potential edge-cases.

AI also played an important role as a drafting assistant. For example, when compressing literature review text into a slide outline, Gemini quickly produced a structured slide flow; still, I adapted the wording to suit our narrative and audience timing. The output was a strong starting point, but the final content required human judgment: removing over-generalized claims, calibrating language for public audiences, and verifying technical claims against HKO documentation.

Throughout this collaboration, I learned to treat AI as a reasoning engine that can offer options and highlight assumptions rather than provide final answers. I learned to craft better prompts; instead of broad questions like “What is simulation?”, I asked targeted prompts like “Given our dataset and target KPI, what stochastic components should a DES include and why?” This produced more useful, narrower responses. Ethically, I was conscious of data governance and privacy; when dealing with sensitive datasets or draft communication, I anonymized data or avoided sharing raw personal information in prompts.

A major technical skill I developed was validating AI output systematically: test-driven checks for code, triangulating LLM claims with peer-reviewed literature or official HKO criteria, and comparing model outputs with baseline expectations. The course taught me that AI expedites workflows but requires human-led validation to produce robust public-policy analysis.

